<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  kidnix: choosing the grown-up PIN.

  /etc/kidnix/parent.toml is root-owned and the shell runs as `kid`, which is
  the whole reason the grown-up gate means anything (a child-writable PIN is
  not a PIN). So the shell's PIN pad cannot save what it collects, and this is
  the action behind the helper that can (/usr/bin/kidnix-set-pin).

  TWO CALLERS, TWO ROUTES, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.

  * A grown-up in their own account (a terminal, or SSH) meets the defaults
    below: allow_active=auth_admin_keep, i.e. a wheel password. That is the
    stock Fedora admin experience, the same shape as org.kidnix.parent-tools,
    and not a new secret to remember.

  * The CHILD'S SESSION is granted this one action outright (YES, no password)
    by /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/40-kidnix-kid.rules, which denies `kid`
    every other "org.kidnix." action; kidnix-export and kidnix-wipe above all.
    It has to be. The image ships parent.toml with no pin_hash, the mandatory
    "choose a grown-up PIN" screen is in the child's session because that is
    the only session this machine ever shows, and the file it must write is
    root-owned. Without the carve-out the chosen PIN lasted until the next
    restart.

  THE AUTHORISATION IS NOT THE AUTHORITY. Saying YES here grants the right to
  *ask*; /usr/bin/kidnix-set-pin decides. It writes only when parent.toml
  carries no hash yet (the first set) or when the caller proved the current
  PIN on stdin; it refuses the reset flag and any path argument to a caller
  who is not root or in wheel; and it rate-limits wrong-PIN attempts. So a
  child can set the first PIN on a machine nobody has set up (which beats no
  PIN at all) and can never change one afterwards. docs/spikes/pin-flow.md is
  the threat model, including the residual "child alone with a brand-new
  laptop" case.

  A parent who forgets the PIN runs `sudo kidnix-set-pin` with the reset flag
  from their own account, which needs wheel and therefore is not reachable
  from here.

  NOTE FOR EDITORS: this is an XML comment, so it may not contain a double
  hyphen anywhere. `kidnix-set-pin` option names are therefore spelled out in
  words above. tests/image/test_lockdown.sh parses this file to make sure a
  well-meant edit cannot unregister the action.
-->
<policyconfig>
  <vendor>kidnix</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/mattcree/kidnix</vendor_url>

  <action id="org.kidnix.set-pin">
    <description>Set the grown-up PIN</description>
    <description xml:lang="en_GB">Set the grown-up PIN</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to change the grown-up PIN on this machine</message>
    <message xml:lang="en_GB">Authentication is required to change the grown-up PIN on this machine</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/bin/kidnix-set-pin</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui">true</annotate>
  </action>
</policyconfig>
